RED TEAM ROUND 2 — sneaky/real-world (live)
DATE: 2026-08-15

TEST A — REAL messy OCR (Gautamiya Tantra, archive.org, 515KB):
  Result: clean=True, RAW_SANSKRIT, density 0.84, 6616 danda markers
  Analysis: hits 17/17 valid Sanskrit words + verse markers → this is GENUINELY USABLE
  Sanskrit (real body, messy margins). Pipeline CORRECTLY accepts it. NOT a vuln.

TEST B — mojibake (my attempt to break via GBK/latin-1 mislabel):
  Result: round-trip recovered the original Devanagari (160811 chars) → clean=True
  Analysis: TEST WAS FLAWED — the latin-1 write + utf-8 read round-tripped back to
  Devanagari. Not a real mojibake. Not a vuln.

TEST C — noise-diluted (72KB, 23% Devanagari + 77% ASCII page noise):
  Result: clean=True (density 0.231 > 0.05 threshold)
  FINDING (LOW-MED): density threshold catches pure garbage but NOT noise-diluted text.
  77% page-header noise would contaminate translation. Accepted as CLEAN_ETEXT.
  Mitigation: assess routes to SCHOLAR_QUEUE because identity=UNRESOLVED (the real safety net).

TEST D — assess on all adversarial inputs:
  Result: NO CRASH on any (fail-closed). All → CLEAN_ETEXT/RAW_SANSKRIT/SCHOLAR_QUEUE.

KEY INSIGHT — DEFENSE IN DEPTH:
  The clean/density check is weak against noise-diluted and garbage-Devanagari.
  BUT the IDENTITY gate (unknown → SCHOLAR_QUEUE, never auto-route to TRANSLATE)
  is the real protector. Garbage can't reach translation unless it has an
  EXACT/PROBABLE identity — which requires a real atlas match.
  → The pipeline is SAFE from garbage reaching translation, even where clean-check is imperfect.

RECOMMENDATION:
  - Consider a word-validity signal (real Sanskrit-word coverage) for the clean check
    to catch noise-diluted Devanagari (Test C). LOW priority — the identity gate already protects.
